\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Head-to-head performance of \texttt  {RhizAeon}\xspace  versus 3SEQ across the 3,200-trial Posada \& Crandall benchmark ($N = 10$, $L = 1{,}000$ nt). (A) Alignment-level detection power across population recombination parameters ($\rho = 4N_e r \in [0, 64]$) and mutational densities ($\theta = 4N_e\mu \in [10, 200]$). Both methods achieve identical power trajectories and reach $98.0\%$ at $\theta = 200$. (B) Inferred event cardinality ($\hat  {K}$) compared against theoretical expectations ($\mathbb  {E}[R]$, black dashed line). Triplet scanning overcounts events up to 10-fold ($\hat  {K} = 16.3$), whereas \texttt  {RhizAeon}\xspace  consolidates shared segments into calibrated historical events ($\hat  {K} \approx 2.1\text  {--}4.6$). (C) Empirical false positive rate (FPR, \%) across 1,200 non-recombinant alignments under Gamma rate heterogeneity ($\alpha \in \{2.0, 0.5, 0.05\}$). Both methods maintain strict control below the 5\% ceiling (red dashed line), where \texttt  {RhizAeon}\xspace  averages $0.58\%$ ($7 / 1{,}200$) and 3SEQ averages $0.17\%$ ($2 / 1{,}200$).\relax }}{4}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:posada_benchmark}{{1}{4}{Head-to-head performance of \RhizAeon versus 3SEQ across the 3,200-trial Posada \& Crandall benchmark ($N = 10$, $L = 1{,}000$ nt). (A) Alignment-level detection power across population recombination parameters ($\rho = 4N_e r \in [0, 64]$) and mutational densities ($\theta = 4N_e\mu \in [10, 200]$). Both methods achieve identical power trajectories and reach $98.0\%$ at $\theta = 200$. (B) Inferred event cardinality ($\hat {K}$) compared against theoretical expectations ($\mathbb {E}[R]$, black dashed line). Triplet scanning overcounts events up to 10-fold ($\hat {K} = 16.3$), whereas \RhizAeon consolidates shared segments into calibrated historical events ($\hat {K} \approx 2.1\text {--}4.6$). (C) Empirical false positive rate (FPR, \%) across 1,200 non-recombinant alignments under Gamma rate heterogeneity ($\alpha \in \{2.0, 0.5, 0.05\}$). Both methods maintain strict control below the 5\% ceiling (red dashed line), where \RhizAeon averages $0.58\%$ ($7 / 1{,}200$) and 3SEQ averages $0.17\%$ ($2 / 1{,}200$).\relax }{figure.caption.1}{}}
