\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Operational performance across specialized genomic and epidemiological regimes. Each row depicts the simulation topology (Col.\nobreakspace  {}1), primary diagnostic metric (Col.\nobreakspace  {}2), boundary precision (Col.\nobreakspace  {}3), and inferred cardinality ($\hat  {K}$) across descendant swarm sizes ($K$, Col.\nobreakspace  {}4). (A)\nobreakspace  {}Epidemic Clonal Expansion: An ancestral reticulation ($P_1, P_2 \to R_{\mathrm  {anc}}$) generates a 1.5\nobreakspace  {}kb cassette in $K \in \{2\text  {--}15\}$ isolates. While 3SEQ inflates events ($\hat  {K} = 9.5\text  {--}55.0$) and scales linearly, \texttt  {RhizAeon}\xspace  collapses swarms into a single event ($\hat  {K} = 1.00$) with $99.9\%$ precision in flat runtime ($491\text  {--}826$\nobreakspace  {}ms). (B)\nobreakspace  {}Multi-Way Mosaic Swarms: Dual Cassette, Nested Doll, and Triple Crossover architectures ($K_{\mathrm  {donor}} = 2.0$). Trajectory deconvolution recovers both parental donors ($1.97/2.00$) with high boundary overlap ($J = 0.86\text  {--}0.98$), whereas closed-world triplet scanning misses secondary donors and inflates events up to $\hat  {K} = 46.5$. (C)\nobreakspace  {}Ghost Donor Introgression: Incomplete taxon sampling across Surrogate Sister, Pure Ghost, and Deep Reservoir regimes. 3SEQ misidentifies background parent $P_2$ as donor in $100\%$ of trials. \texttt  {RhizAeon}\xspace  maintains $\le 6\%$ false framing and attributes $98.3\%$ of surrogate donors correctly. (D)\nobreakspace  {}Bacterial Micro-Conversions: Short replacement tracts ($L_{\mathrm  {tract}} \in [50, 800]$\nobreakspace  {}nt). Continuous dyadic wavelets achieve $40.3\%$ power at 50\nobreakspace  {}nt ($d = 0.10$, vs.\nobreakspace  {}3SEQ $5.2\%$) and $89.0\%$ at 100\nobreakspace  {}nt, with $0.00\%$ background false alarms. \relax }}{15}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:specialized_scenarios}{{2}{15}{Operational performance across specialized genomic and epidemiological regimes. Each row depicts the simulation topology (Col.~1), primary diagnostic metric (Col.~2), boundary precision (Col.~3), and inferred cardinality ($\hat {K}$) across descendant swarm sizes ($K$, Col.~4). (A)~Epidemic Clonal Expansion: An ancestral reticulation ($P_1, P_2 \to R_{\mathrm {anc}}$) generates a 1.5~kb cassette in $K \in \{2\text {--}15\}$ isolates. While 3SEQ inflates events ($\hat {K} = 9.5\text {--}55.0$) and scales linearly, \RhizAeon collapses swarms into a single event ($\hat {K} = 1.00$) with $99.9\%$ precision in flat runtime ($491\text {--}826$~ms). (B)~Multi-Way Mosaic Swarms: Dual Cassette, Nested Doll, and Triple Crossover architectures ($K_{\mathrm {donor}} = 2.0$). Trajectory deconvolution recovers both parental donors ($1.97/2.00$) with high boundary overlap ($J = 0.86\text {--}0.98$), whereas closed-world triplet scanning misses secondary donors and inflates events up to $\hat {K} = 46.5$. (C)~Ghost Donor Introgression: Incomplete taxon sampling across Surrogate Sister, Pure Ghost, and Deep Reservoir regimes. 3SEQ misidentifies background parent $P_2$ as donor in $100\%$ of trials. \RhizAeon maintains $\le 6\%$ false framing and attributes $98.3\%$ of surrogate donors correctly. (D)~Bacterial Micro-Conversions: Short replacement tracts ($L_{\mathrm {tract}} \in [50, 800]$~nt). Continuous dyadic wavelets achieve $40.3\%$ power at 50~nt ($d = 0.10$, vs.~3SEQ $5.2\%$) and $89.0\%$ at 100~nt, with $0.00\%$ background false alarms. \relax }{figure.caption.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Sarbecovirus whole-genome recombination profiles, Spike mosaicism, and ancestral recombination graphs. (A) Genome-wide breakpoint flux $\Phi _{\mathrm  {bp}}(u)$ across 83 sarbecoviruses ($30.9$\nobreakspace  {}kb) aligned to gene annotations. Recombination hotspots concentrate at the Spike $5'$ NTD junction (21,825\nobreakspace  {}bp), codon 313 hinge (22,764\nobreakspace  {}bp), S1/S2 furin site (23,618\nobreakspace  {}bp), and ORF8/N cluster (28,810\nobreakspace  {}bp). Coldspots protect the RdRp catalytic core (nt 13,400--16,200) and Spike S2 fusion machinery. Beneath the genes, \texttt  {RhizAeon}\xspace  changepoints (orange) match 21 published GARD breakpoints (blue) with 99.0\nobreakspace  {}nt median deviation. (B) Reticulate Spike glycoprotein mosaicism (nt 21,000--26,000). Rather than SARS-CoV-2 emerging as a terminal recombinant chimera, Wuhan-Hu-1 and Laotian bat isolate BANAL-52 exhibit continuous collinearity across the entire Spike glycoprotein (blue, $\sim 5\%$ divergence). In contrast, Yunnan bat isolate RaTG13 harbors a divergent recombinant cassette specifically within the receptor-binding domain (RBD, codons 314--540, purple, divergence spiking to $13.8\%$), acquired from an unsampled bat reservoir. (C) Segmental incongruence across codon 313 in a two-partition tanglegram. Wuhan-Hu-1, BANAL-52, and RaTG13 cluster sister in the NTD (C1). In the RBD (C2), Wuhan-Hu-1 and BANAL-52 remain tightly clustered, while RaTG13 diverges basally into an outgroup position. (D) Polarized Ancestral Recombination Graph (ARG). Reticulation arcs trace the introgression of the divergent RBD cassette into RaTG13 from an unsampled bat reservoir ($Z = 20.9, P = 2.1 \times 10^{-9}$), while the ancestral SARS-CoV-2 / BANAL-52 trunk preserved the human-compatible ACE2 contact ridge, alongside NTD micro-conversions from Thai RacCS203 into BANAL-247. The tripartite ARG reduces residual strain by $74.0\%$ ($\Delta \mathrm  {BIC} = 0.0$). \relax }}{16}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sarbecovirus_recombination_landscape}{{3}{16}{Sarbecovirus whole-genome recombination profiles, Spike mosaicism, and ancestral recombination graphs. (A) Genome-wide breakpoint flux $\Phi _{\mathrm {bp}}(u)$ across 83 sarbecoviruses ($30.9$~kb) aligned to gene annotations. Recombination hotspots concentrate at the Spike $5'$ NTD junction (21,825~bp), codon 313 hinge (22,764~bp), S1/S2 furin site (23,618~bp), and ORF8/N cluster (28,810~bp). Coldspots protect the RdRp catalytic core (nt 13,400--16,200) and Spike S2 fusion machinery. Beneath the genes, \RhizAeon changepoints (orange) match 21 published GARD breakpoints (blue) with 99.0~nt median deviation. (B) Reticulate Spike glycoprotein mosaicism (nt 21,000--26,000). Rather than SARS-CoV-2 emerging as a terminal recombinant chimera, Wuhan-Hu-1 and Laotian bat isolate BANAL-52 exhibit continuous collinearity across the entire Spike glycoprotein (blue, $\sim 5\%$ divergence). In contrast, Yunnan bat isolate RaTG13 harbors a divergent recombinant cassette specifically within the receptor-binding domain (RBD, codons 314--540, purple, divergence spiking to $13.8\%$), acquired from an unsampled bat reservoir. (C) Segmental incongruence across codon 313 in a two-partition tanglegram. Wuhan-Hu-1, BANAL-52, and RaTG13 cluster sister in the NTD (C1). In the RBD (C2), Wuhan-Hu-1 and BANAL-52 remain tightly clustered, while RaTG13 diverges basally into an outgroup position. (D) Polarized Ancestral Recombination Graph (ARG). Reticulation arcs trace the introgression of the divergent RBD cassette into RaTG13 from an unsampled bat reservoir ($Z = 20.9, P = 2.1 \times 10^{-9}$), while the ancestral SARS-CoV-2 / BANAL-52 trunk preserved the human-compatible ACE2 contact ridge, alongside NTD micro-conversions from Thai RacCS203 into BANAL-247. The tripartite ARG reduces residual strain by $74.0\%$ ($\Delta \mathrm {BIC} = 0.0$). \relax }{figure.caption.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Whole-chromosome recombination deconvolution, antimicrobial resistance mosaicism, and ancestral recombination graphs in \textit  {Streptococcus pneumoniae} PMEN1. (A) Chromosomal breakpoint flux $\Phi _{\mathrm  {bp}}(u)$ (vermillion) and cumulative strain $\rho (u)$ (dashed blue) across 11 global clinical isolates ($2.22$\nobreakspace  {}Mb, Spain23F-1 / PMEN1). Six hotspots emerge: $pbp2x/cps$ (285--352\nobreakspace  {}kb), 732\nobreakspace  {}kb, 1.15\nobreakspace  {}Mb, \textit  {folA} (1,620--1,649\nobreakspace  {}kb), 1.72\nobreakspace  {}Mb, and \textit  {pspC/cbpA} (2,105--2,138\nobreakspace  {}kb). Tickmarks benchmark \texttt  {RhizAeon}\xspace  changepoints (orange) against 52 published Gubbins boundaries (blue, $96.2\%$ concordance $\le 2$\nobreakspace  {}kb, median deviation 92.5\nobreakspace  {}nt). (B) AMR and capsular switch mosaicism (nt 270,000--370,000). Ancestral isolate 2097 carries the core backbone (dark blue). Isolate 1731 displays the 1,121\nobreakspace  {}bp active-site \textit  {pbp2x} transpeptidase replacement ($P = 9.85 \times 10^{-35}$), isolate 2682 captures a 73.9\nobreakspace  {}kb \textit  {pbp2x-cps} co-transfer mega-cassette, and isolate 3355 reveals serotype 23F $\to $ 19A capsule replacement driving PCV7 vaccine escape. (C) Segmental incongruence across the \textit  {pbp2x} catalytic domain. In the core chromosome, all isolates form a monophyletic complex, whereas in \textit  {pbp2x}, isolates 1731 and 2682 diverge into commensal viridans streptococci (\textit  {S.\nobreakspace  {}mitis} / \textit  {S.\nobreakspace  {}oralis}, $\Delta d > 6\%$). (D) Polarized ARG for PMEN1 clinical adaptation. Arcs reconcile \textit  {pbp2x} active-site import, 73.9\nobreakspace  {}kb co-transfer, and 19A capsular replacement onto the Spain23F-1 backbone. \relax }}{17}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:spneumoniae_chromosome_landscape}{{4}{17}{Whole-chromosome recombination deconvolution, antimicrobial resistance mosaicism, and ancestral recombination graphs in \textit {Streptococcus pneumoniae} PMEN1. (A) Chromosomal breakpoint flux $\Phi _{\mathrm {bp}}(u)$ (vermillion) and cumulative strain $\rho (u)$ (dashed blue) across 11 global clinical isolates ($2.22$~Mb, Spain23F-1 / PMEN1). Six hotspots emerge: $pbp2x/cps$ (285--352~kb), 732~kb, 1.15~Mb, \textit {folA} (1,620--1,649~kb), 1.72~Mb, and \textit {pspC/cbpA} (2,105--2,138~kb). Tickmarks benchmark \RhizAeon changepoints (orange) against 52 published Gubbins boundaries (blue, $96.2\%$ concordance $\le 2$~kb, median deviation 92.5~nt). (B) AMR and capsular switch mosaicism (nt 270,000--370,000). Ancestral isolate 2097 carries the core backbone (dark blue). Isolate 1731 displays the 1,121~bp active-site \textit {pbp2x} transpeptidase replacement ($P = 9.85 \times 10^{-35}$), isolate 2682 captures a 73.9~kb \textit {pbp2x-cps} co-transfer mega-cassette, and isolate 3355 reveals serotype 23F $\to $ 19A capsule replacement driving PCV7 vaccine escape. (C) Segmental incongruence across the \textit {pbp2x} catalytic domain. In the core chromosome, all isolates form a monophyletic complex, whereas in \textit {pbp2x}, isolates 1731 and 2682 diverge into commensal viridans streptococci (\textit {S.~mitis} / \textit {S.~oralis}, $\Delta d > 6\%$). (D) Polarized ARG for PMEN1 clinical adaptation. Arcs reconcile \textit {pbp2x} active-site import, 73.9~kb co-transfer, and 19A capsular replacement onto the Spain23F-1 backbone. \relax }{figure.caption.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Whole-gene recombination deconvolution, ectopic micro-conversion, and ancestral recombination graphs in Hominoidea MHC Class\nobreakspace  {}I. (A) Breakpoint flux $\Phi _{\mathrm  {bp}}(u)$ (vermillion) and cumulative strain $\rho (u)$ (dashed blue) across $N = 379$ primate alleles ($1{,}128$\nobreakspace  {}bp, Exons 1--7). Reticulations concentrate in the antigen recognition site (ARS, Exons 2--3, nt 72--618) and transmembrane anchor (Exon 5), while Exon 4 ($\alpha _3$ stalk) remains a conserved coldspot ($93.7\%$ of strain in ARS and TM). The unsupervised boundary inflection at nt 608--615 matches the 618\nobreakspace  {}bp Exon 3/4 junction. (B) Reticulate mosaic architecture in primate MHC Class\nobreakspace  {}I chimeras. \textit  {HLA-B*46:01} is deconvolved as an inter-locus chimera carrying an exact 73\nobreakspace  {}bp micro-conversion (nt 220--293, codons 66--76, green) from \textit  {HLA-C*01:02} within an \textit  {HLA-B*15:01} backbone (dark blue), transferring the C1 functional epitope that binds NK cell receptors KIR2DL2/3. (C) Segmental incongruence across the $\alpha _3$ boundary. In Exon 4, \textit  {HLA-A}, \textit  {B}, and \textit  {C} form monophyletic clades (\textit  {B*46:01} sister to \textit  {B*15:01}). In the Exon 2 C1 cassette, \textit  {B*46:01} translocates into the \textit  {HLA-C} clade sister to \textit  {C*01:02}. (D) Polarized ARG for primate MHC. Reticulation arcs anchor the 73\nobreakspace  {}bp C1 micro-conversion and ancient chimpanzee \textit  {Patr-AL*01} transfers onto the tandem gene duplication backbone. \relax }}{18}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hominoidea_mhc_landscape}{{5}{18}{Whole-gene recombination deconvolution, ectopic micro-conversion, and ancestral recombination graphs in Hominoidea MHC Class~I. (A) Breakpoint flux $\Phi _{\mathrm {bp}}(u)$ (vermillion) and cumulative strain $\rho (u)$ (dashed blue) across $N = 379$ primate alleles ($1{,}128$~bp, Exons 1--7). Reticulations concentrate in the antigen recognition site (ARS, Exons 2--3, nt 72--618) and transmembrane anchor (Exon 5), while Exon 4 ($\alpha _3$ stalk) remains a conserved coldspot ($93.7\%$ of strain in ARS and TM). The unsupervised boundary inflection at nt 608--615 matches the 618~bp Exon 3/4 junction. (B) Reticulate mosaic architecture in primate MHC Class~I chimeras. \textit {HLA-B*46:01} is deconvolved as an inter-locus chimera carrying an exact 73~bp micro-conversion (nt 220--293, codons 66--76, green) from \textit {HLA-C*01:02} within an \textit {HLA-B*15:01} backbone (dark blue), transferring the C1 functional epitope that binds NK cell receptors KIR2DL2/3. (C) Segmental incongruence across the $\alpha _3$ boundary. In Exon 4, \textit {HLA-A}, \textit {B}, and \textit {C} form monophyletic clades (\textit {B*46:01} sister to \textit {B*15:01}). In the Exon 2 C1 cassette, \textit {B*46:01} translocates into the \textit {HLA-C} clade sister to \textit {C*01:02}. (D) Polarized ARG for primate MHC. Reticulation arcs anchor the 73~bp C1 micro-conversion and ancient chimpanzee \textit {Patr-AL*01} transfers onto the tandem gene duplication backbone. \relax }{figure.caption.9}{}}
